Root cause located: the problem is NOT with AS1, AS2, or ACM. It is a local firewall rule on the Uni gateway router itself. Specifically, iptables has explicit DROP rules for the entire 198.82.0.0/24 prefix (which contains acm.org's 198.82.0.1):

  OUTPUT  chain: DROP all -> 198.82.0.0/24  (matched 69 packets / 5772 bytes)
  FORWARD chain: DROP all -> 198.82.0.0/24  (matched 8 packets / 552 bytes — your laptop's pings)

How we localized it: AS1's tcpdump on its Uni-facing interface showed ZERO echo requests for 198.82.0.1 arriving during a 20-packet ping window, while sanity pings from Uni to all of AS1's interfaces succeeded. That proved packets to 198.82.0.1 were never leaving Uni. Local check then revealed the iptables DROPs with non-zero hit counters exactly matching our test traffic.

Proposed fix: remove the two DROP rules for 198.82.0.0/24 from the OUTPUT and FORWARD chains on the Uni gateway.

Why I am NOT applying this unilaterally: the rules appear to be an administrator-installed policy (they affect ALL university users, touch a security boundary, and may represent an intentional block of acm.org). Per policy, such changes require university network administrator approval.

Status: administrators have been notified; awaiting their decision. Until they act, acm.org will remain unreachable from the university network.
